Git truncates function annotations to 80 bytes. This scan requires a complete credential component, so a longer component survives when quoted in a hunk header. The ordinary privacy filters also miss it because the complete registered credential is absent. A real Git fixture confirmed this output with no hidden indices. Exact targeted reproduction:
```sh
bun - <<'TS'
import {readFileSync} from 'node:fs';
import {normalizedLayers} from './src/gates/private.ts';
const s=readFileSync('src/integrations/mcp-change.ts','utf8');
const f=s.slice(s.indexOf('function privatePatchLines('),s.indexOf('\nfunction catalogueKey('));
const scan=new Function('normalizedLayers',new Bun.Transpiler({loader:'ts'}).transformSync(f)+';return privatePatchLines;')(normalizedLayers);
const beta='beta-secret-'.padEnd(132,'b');
if(!scan('@@ -8,4 +8,4 @@ '+beta.slice(0,80)+'\n \n-public old\n+public new\n',['alpha-secret\n'+beta]).has(0)) throw Error('credential annotation survives');
TS
```
Output: `error: credential annotation survives` (exit 1).

The guard runs only once before remoteSh, but remoteSh uses withGitHubRetry, which awaits failed commands and backoff before issuing another push without invoking the guard. The requested claim protection therefore does not cover push retries. A read-only probe using the actual pushExistingBranch and retry source with mocked transport reproduced one guard invocation and two push attempts after ownership changed.

